23:08:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It's worth looking at what those studies actually measure, because the picture is more complicated than "men score lower on empathy, therefore they're self-centered." **Self-report vs. performance-based measures show very different results.** When men and women are asked to rate their own empathy on questionnaires ("I often feel sympathy for others' problems"), women tend to report higher scores. But when researchers use actual performance-based tests — like reading emotions from facial expressions or tone of voice (the "Reading the Mind in the Eyes" test, for example) — the gap shrinks dramatically, and sometimes disappears, depending on the study. This suggests part of the difference is about *self-perception and social expectations* around expressing empathy, not necessarily men's actual capacity to understand or feel others' emotions. **Context and motivation change results a lot.** Some studies show men perform just as well on empathy-related tasks when they're specifically told empathic accuracy is being tested and matters, or when there's a clear incentive. This points to empathy being something that's expressed differently or selectively, not simply absent. **Empathy ≠ selfishness.** Even taking the self-report gap at face value, "scores lower on a self-reported empathy questionnaire" is a different claim from "is fundamentally self-centered and doesn't care about others' needs." Someone can have somewhat lower scores on standard empathy measures and still be a caring, attentive partner — and someone can score high on empathy questionnaires and still act selfishly in practice (empathy and behavior aren't perfectly linked). **Effect sizes matter, and they're usually described as small-to-moderate, not "men have none and women have lots."** Overlap between the sexes is substantial — meaning many men score higher than many women, and vice versa. So there is real research showing an average difference on certain empathy measures, particularly self-report ones — that part is true, I don't want to pretend otherwise. But it doesn't cleanly back up "men are self-centered" as a fixed, universal trait — it's a more specific, more modest, more context-dependent finding than that, and it doesn't tell you much about any one man, including your boyfriend.
